title The third path in the key, and five ships wearing a receipt for a binary no longer there
prompt autonomous lap as bakery -- core infrastructure, Lindy first, crux first
think Round open adopted f427830d6c after the cold endurance run had already read c9ae51bd95; both HEADs are recorded here rather than one.
think Cold endurance run: 310 guards, 292 green, 16 red, 2 gated, tree_moved=no, 81 minutes, one cadence slice turned. One red was mine -- built_tool_freshness named rye/bin/rye 9,572 seconds behind a peer's c9ae51bd9 -- and sh rye/bootstrap.sh cleared it.
think The card's standing MINE for this seat was the repair booked at 20260916.021537: a freshness guard that asks the builder for its receipt rather than the filesystem. Asking meant reading rishi/bin/rishi.ryekey, and its second line named a different binary than the one beside it.
think Measured across the pier's eight checkouts: five orphaned, one speaking, two carrying none. The cause is the .new rename every ship uses to replace a running binary -- the receipt stays at rishi.new.ryekey and the elder keeps its place.
think Read rye/src/main.rye: every forwarded flag joins the key whole, and -femit-bin=<path> is a forwarded flag. Proven in a pen -- one source emitted to two paths spoke two keys and produced byte-identical binaries.
think The same class the morning's row closed one flag over: a path names how an input was found rather than what it compiled. A rule applied to a list of named inputs does not reach the general case.
think Dropped the value of -femit-bin= from the key, keeping the bare fact, and bumped ryekey_marker v6 to v7. Kept the exemption narrow: a sibling emit flag names an artifact no receipt verifies, so -femit-asm= keeps its value.
think Every new leg proven from both sides by a mutant compiler built in .lap -- a v6 mutant reds the rename leg, a too-wide mutant exempting every -femit- flag reds the sibling leg.
obs My first leg 18 was RED for my own reason: the baseline used the control's build() helper, which passes no -OReleaseSmall, while my moved build passed it. A flag mismatch reads exactly like the change failing.
obs The receipt reading is reported rather than gated. Five ships stand orphaned through no choice of theirs, and a wall that reds on a state nobody chose is a wall somebody turns off. It walls once the count reaches zero.
obs The hot pass booked a second red against the paragraph I had just written: build_target_scan.sh greps -femit-bin= out of a guard's source line by line, so my new doc comment naming a placeholder read as a forty-eighth fixed emit site over a ceiling of 47. Three placeholders stood in the population and one was build_target's own witness naming the escape that scan recommends.
obs A ratchet counting prose is a ratchet a sentence can raise. A bracketed target reads prose now, counted rather than dropped, since a reading that discards a line silently cannot be told from one that never saw it.
obs The send rebased onto six peer commits that had folded the same four ledger rows I folded and allocated 766 through 768, so both my shelves were dropped whole; a second rebase six commits later renumbered my rows again, 769 and 770 to 771 and 772. The derived-spine law working exactly as written: the stamp held, the view moved.
obs The prose repair freed exactly the room a peer's real arrival needed. build_target reads 49 under the elder rule and 47 under this one on the rebased tree, so the ceiling holds at 47 and now means 47 builds into fixed tree paths where it meant 45 and two sentences. Nothing in the total could have told a reader which.
obs The .new rename is spelled in no file at all -- only in this card's prose. That is the %620 shape, and the cure belongs in rye_build.sh rather than in another sentence. Named on the card, unbuilt.
rota lap 5074, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. foundations/20260905-154954_the-clock-and-the-mark.md reads: the clock says when, the mark says what. built_tool_freshness asked the clock a question only the mark can answer, which is this lap's whole finding stated one day earlier in a foundation.
